verdict.cause:
I reproduced this from a fresh session: Demo access, then C2C-101 in the rail, then Submit, then Open Dispatch. The screen is correct and matches the server. biotech-walkthrough.md section 6 (lines 111-122) was written on 2026-09-21 (commit 948ef3ce98). It describes a gate that has changed since.

GET /api/submissions/sequences/1/dispatch-readiness returns gate.blockers with 4 sentences:
(1) 8 open error-severity validation findings;
(2) 6 leaves point at documents not yet approved;
(3) no completed Shadow Review;
(4) no Part 11 release signature.

The second blocker and the larger error count both come from d155ef0994 (2026-10-08, P-20/P-21/P-22): "approval gates the release, required Module 1 is an error". The 8 errors are 6 MISSING_REQUIRED_SECTION (US Module 1 sections 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.12.14, 1.14.4.2 and 1.20) and 2 IND_MODULE3_REQUIRED (3.2.S and 3.2.P). None of them is about a leaf that cannot be resolved. The DOCUMENT_NOT_APPROVED findings name each Vault document by its UUID, which shows the checker now reads UUID leaves. So the script's admission that "leaves placed from the Vault by UUID are reported as unresolvable" is false today. The presenter would confess to a defect the screen does not show, and would give the wrong counts (3 blockers and six errors, where the screen shows 4 and 8).

The script's opening step, "Open Dispatch readiness", is also stale. The rail has no such entry. The verdict now lives on the project's Submit step and in the Filing card (P-33), and the full sentences are on Submission Center's Dispatch tab.

A second, smaller part of the finding is a product defect (medium). "Dispatch blocked · 4 blockers" counts the server's blocker sentences (ProjectMarkets.tsx:136-137, n = gate.blockers.length). The list directly under it, "What must close before dispatch" (MarketBlockers.tsx:91-112 marketTodos, rendered at :137), breaks those 4 into 16 unnumbered items: 8 placement errors, 6 approvals, Shadow Review and the release signature. The page never says the 16 items make up the 4 blockers, so a buyer sees "4" heading 16 items. The Dispatch tab ("Four things stand between sequence 0000 and dispatch", then 4 sentences) is consistent with the count.

The screens show the data correctly. Two things around them are wrong.

(1) The leaf route changed and the demo seed was never updated to match. Commit d0da50de06 ("The placement vocabulary follows the submission type") made upsertLeaf judge a 510(k) placement against the 'estar' vocabulary. That vocabulary is checked by shape only: slugVerdict in shared/regulatory/placement-vocabulary.ts:335-355, reached from server/services/submission-service/submission-service.ts:4692-4696. So `PUT /api/submissions/sequences/:id/leaves` with sectionCode "estar.device-description" now returns 200 and writes a real leaf. It used to return 400, which is what finding F3 recorded.

The MDX pack's "eSTAR section codes on the leaf route (probe)" step (scripts/demo/launch-demo/packs/mdx.mjs:386-394) sends that PUT with the Device Description vault document. On a 200 it just returns a message, so the leaf stays. The next step (mdx.mjs:396-413) checks for existing leaves by section code only, so it also places the same document at 3.2.P.1. The result is 7 leaves, with the Device Description in two places. The pack only ever adds, so re-running the seed never removes the extra leaf.

(2) The presenter script was never updated. docs/demo/mdx-walkthrough.md:116-118 (§4) and :132-145 (§5), and docs/evidence/DEMO/mdx/README.md:47, 82-83 and 115-145, still describe the old run: the eSTAR id refused (F3), 6 leaves, and six UNRESOLVED_DOCUMENT errors (F5). The committed manifest from the 2026-10-31 run (docs/evidence/DEMO/mdx/manifest.json, commit e7544f93c6) already contradicts them:
- estarLeafProbe.status = 200;
- leaves.count = 7;
- readiness.validationErrors = 0;
- the dispatchQc blockers are "7 leaves point at documents not yet approved" and "No completed Shadow Review".

So §4's "the refusal is in the manifest" is false, and F5 no longer happens on this build.

verdict.minimalFix:
All changes are in server/services/module3Composer.ts.

1) In the '3.1' generator (lines 3125-3131), read each name from its own source type:
```ts
const substances = m.filter((s) => s.sourceType === 'drug_substance');
const products = m.filter((s) => s.sourceType === 'drug_product');
const name = val(substances, 'name');
const productName = val(products, 'name');
const form = val(products, 'dosageFormDescription');
const strength = val(products, 'strength');
```
Then render: `(name ? `The drug substance is ${name}. ` : '') + (productName || form ? `The drug product is ${productName || 'the drug product'}${form ? ` (${[strength, form].filter(Boolean).join(' ')})` : ''}. ` : '')`.

2) Make the same change in the '3.3' generator (line 3161, and the narrative at 3181-3184): substance name from `substances`, product name and form from `products`.

3) Optional, same file, low risk: in the 3.1 table add `['3.3', 'Literature References']`, which this composer always emits. Also correct the misleading comment at 3152-3154. Listing 3.2.A and 3.2.R correctly needs the composed set from composeFullModule3 / module3-convergence-service, so leave that as a follow-up rather than adding static rows.

4) Follow-up, not for today: the completeness count for 3.1/3.3 should require `name` from a drug_substance source specifically. Today the product's name satisfies it (rule at lines 217-218; field union at lines 3284-3292).

Notes:
- No test pins the 3.1 or 3.3 narrative.
- After deploying, the stored text in cmc_module3_sections changes only after a Module 3 recompile or a section refresh (POST /api/cmc/module3-os/sections/:projectId/:sectionKey/refresh). The refresh is a governed write, so it is not something a verifier may do on the demo database. Re-seeding or recompiling the demo project picks it up.
- 3.1 and 3.3 are Draft in the demo data, so no approval is voided.
